## Note — a plan transcription bug, not a consultation

TASK2-PLAN.md section 2.2 defines `polarTex`/`polarAnswer` with four literal
backslashes before `left`/`cos`/`sin`/`right` (verified with `cat -A` on the
raw file — not a Read-tool rendering artifact), e.g.
`` `\\\\left(\\\\cos ... \\\\right)` `` where every other TeX string in this
plan and in the codebase's existing generators uses two backslashes per
command. Copying the plan literally would have produced `\\left(` at
runtime — a TeX line-break command followed by the literal text "left(" —
which is wrong and inconsistent with the plan's own parenthetical two lines
later ("note the space after `\\cos`", two backslashes). Treated this as a
transcription bug in the plan document itself (the same nested-escaping
failure mode CLAUDE.md warns about, here adding a level rather than eating
one) rather than the intended behaviour, and wrote `polarTex`/`polarAnswer`
with the standard doubled-backslash convention instead. The generated-TeX
test suite (KaTeX strict rendering plus the `BARE_TEX_COMMAND` denylist)
passed either way — `\\left(` is valid TeX, so nothing would have caught the
quadruple-backslash version mechanically — so this was a manual read, not a
gate catching it.

